WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.300
Good evening.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:10.080
I'm John Harbison, part
of a large team helping

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.950
to survive the well-deserved
sabbatical of Fred Harris.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:24.520
And we are here to give audible
thanks for his leadership

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.600
and to present some
pieces of unusual cast.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.450
This is the first
ever FJE concert

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.830
consisting entirely of
arrangements and compositions

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:40.540
by present and former
members of the band.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.690
It is also the
first presentation

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:50.200
of the group in living memory
with the absence of Fred

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.310
Harris--

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.650
a near impossible feat.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.230
We offer the concert
in his honor,

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.810
of course-- in his spirit.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.310
The well-oiled
Fred-less machine has

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.980
been kept humming through
the rehearsal period,

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.810
mainly through the artistic
and diplomatic savvy

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.500
of Peter Goddard, our pianist.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.129
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.230
Only a few of us tonight
ever heard the band

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.500
under Herb Pomeroy,
but among those would

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.310
be our guest
conductor tonight, who

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.100
has been close to the
band for many, many years

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.440
as player, composer,
and arranger.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.140
He was the first
leader after Herb.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.020
We're especially glad to be
hearing four of his tunes

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.260
tonight.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.730
Please join us in
welcoming Jamshied Sharifi.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:06.148
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:44.961 --> 00:08:47.456
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:13.903 --> 00:09:16.398
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:26.757 --> 00:10:28.753
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:48.016 --> 00:13:50.008
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:53.752 --> 00:14:56.242
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:04.600
Thank you.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:08.650
You heard from Baba
Ogunlade on trumpet and--

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:09.700
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.520
Zhi Wei Gan--

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.545
Zhi Wei Gan also on trumpet.

00:15:14.545 --> 00:15:17.020
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:20.800
Before that, we heard from
Julia Fiksinski [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:25.990
and Tony Terrasa and a little
bit from Peter Godart as well.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.780
It's a great pleasure
to be here again.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:33.740
As some of you know, I used
to do this in another life.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:36.530
I started conducting
this band in 1985,

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.400
which sounds prehistoric--
before most of these people

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:39.940
were born.

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:42.910
In fact, I was about their
age when that happened.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:47.890
So it's been a great
delight to do this again,

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:54.040
to be back here again, mostly to
be in front of these musicians,

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:57.460
because they're really
phenomenal both musically

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.380
and as human beings.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:04.150
And it's just a lovely,
lovely experience to be here.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:06.760
As John Harbison
pointed out, we're

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:10.300
doing music that's all
coming from inside the band,

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:14.980
and some of it is very
much student directed.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.070
The next piece, which is
written by Tony Terrasa,

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:23.980
will also be conducted by him,
and it features him, I think.

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:25.956
Who else is playing on this?

00:16:25.956 --> 00:16:27.690
We've got Kevin and
we've got Julia.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:29.760
Oh, OK, Julia again
will be playing,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.760
and Kevin Costello
will play trumbone.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.018
This is Fluffy.

00:16:35.018 --> 00:16:37.513
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:41.006 --> 00:16:43.002
[SNAPPING]

00:16:44.499 --> 00:16:47.493
One, two, one, two, three, four.

00:16:47.493 --> 00:16:49.489
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:20:01.226 --> 00:20:03.721
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:29.429 --> 00:22:31.924
[APPLAUSE AND CHEERS]

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:56.390
We're going to continue with a
piece written by our guitarist,

00:22:56.390 --> 00:23:00.500
Richard Oates, for a smaller
ensemble, a chamber ensemble,

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.739
called Insignificant Figures.

00:23:36.671 --> 00:23:39.166
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:27:17.229 --> 00:27:19.724
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:24.474
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:39.920
Thank you again.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:44.100
That was Richard's piece,
Insignificant Figures.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:45.950
We're going to
finish with a piece

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:50.000
by Peter Godart who
plays piano and has also

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:53.630
been a part of the team
running the band during Fred's

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:54.770
sabbatical.

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:56.480
As you know, Fred is--

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:58.400
or as you may know,
Fred is writing a book.

00:29:58.400 --> 00:29:59.300
Can I tell you?

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:00.050
Can I tell this?

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:01.470
Is this a secret?

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:02.930
Yes, you can.

00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:03.430
Go ahead.

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:03.970
Oh, I can.

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:05.080
OK, good.

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:08.980
Fred is writing a book about
the Lion of Boston jazz,

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:12.400
Herb Pomeroy, who,
as you may know,

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:17.280
was a really
amazing jazz figure.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:19.470
He played with Charlie
Parker, with Stan Kenton,

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.510
with Lionel Hampton.

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.340
He was a fixture
at Berkeley, and he

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:26.850
also was really the
founder of this band

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:29.680
and ran it for 22 years.

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:32.760
So I'm really looking
forward to that book.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:35.670
Peter's been a big part of
keeping this team together.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:39.000
This next piece that he wrote
will feature, well, a lot of us

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.510
in the band, but
Talia Khan as well.

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:44.322
[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.287
And it's called Wisdom.

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.996
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:32:42.998 --> 00:32:46.038
(SINGING) Searching for
wisdom in the mirror.

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:52.215
Can't see past yourself to
get a clearer point of view.

00:32:52.215 --> 00:32:57.165
Don't you find it
ain't that mirror?

00:32:57.165 --> 00:33:06.570
[INAUDIBLE] understand
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:20.130
Will never understand.

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:25.130
[INAUDIBLE] just like a dancer.

00:33:25.130 --> 00:33:29.050
[INAUDIBLE] again.

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:30.879
Don't you worry, though.

00:33:30.879 --> 00:33:34.906
He probably can't control
his nature [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:34.906 --> 00:33:37.396
[JAZZ MUSIC]

00:35:33.701 --> 00:35:36.191
[WORDLESS SINGING]

00:41:04.534 --> 00:41:10.023
(SINGING) Seek for
wisdom in the mirror.

00:41:10.023 --> 00:41:14.023
Can't see past yourself to
get a clearer point of view.

00:41:14.023 --> 00:41:18.733
Don't you find it
ain't that mirror?

00:41:18.733 --> 00:41:22.757
Wisdom takes its time and
there is nothing you can do.

00:41:22.757 --> 00:41:25.142
Get outside your head.

00:41:25.142 --> 00:41:29.435
In time you'll understand though
you know it's a true thing.

00:41:29.435 --> 00:41:32.310
A universal true thing.

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:35.310
The universal true thing, you.

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:38.793
The universal true thing, you.

00:41:38.793 --> 00:41:41.248
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:58.960 --> 00:42:05.260
Thank you very much Talia Khan,
Brandon Allen, Kevin Costello,

00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:08.970
Peter Godart's piece, "Wisdom."

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:15.352
We will be back with another set
of great music from the band.

00:42:15.352 --> 00:42:16.560
Thank you so much for coming.

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.466
We'll see you soon.

00:42:17.466 --> 00:42:17.966
Bye.

00:42:17.966 --> 00:42:19.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:19.370 --> 00:42:26.468
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:26.468 --> 00:42:28.963
[JAZZ MUSIC]

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:25.670
Thank you, Richard
Oates, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:52:25.670 --> 00:52:28.430
We're going to
continue with a piece

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:32.810
by a dear friend of mine,
Markus Sieber, who has

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:35.480
a project that he calls Aukai.

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:38.810
And this is not music
that one would typically

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:40.310
hear played by a big band.

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:43.460
But I was intrigued
by his records

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:47.570
and wanted to try a
version of one of his songs

00:52:47.570 --> 00:52:48.320
with this band.

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:51.190
And they're doing
a fantastic job.

00:52:51.190 --> 00:52:53.200
This is "Soplo del Sueno."

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:26.627
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:54.640
Switch it on, please.

00:58:54.640 --> 00:58:56.980
Markus Sieber's
"Soplo del Sueno."

00:59:02.930 --> 00:59:06.110
We're going to continue with a
piece written by our own Kevin

00:59:06.110 --> 00:59:08.080
Costello, yes.

00:59:08.080 --> 00:59:11.750
There's some argument
over the title, right?

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:13.850
It's in the program
as "Alexis Dream."

00:59:13.850 --> 00:59:15.000
We'll just go with that.

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:16.410
We'll just go with that, OK.

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:19.200
It has also been called
"Alexis's Dream."

00:59:19.200 --> 00:59:21.540
But whatever works.

00:59:21.540 --> 00:59:22.400
Oh, yes.

00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:24.900
And there are a couple of people
who are not in the program.

00:59:24.900 --> 00:59:27.480
Isn't that correct?

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:30.900
We are very blessed to
have Evan Ziporyn join us

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:31.980
on clarinet tonight.

00:59:31.980 --> 00:59:34.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:38.130
He is an absolute
clarinet luminary.

00:59:38.130 --> 00:59:42.030
But he made the time
to learn the music

00:59:42.030 --> 00:59:44.100
and sit in with us,
which is wonderful.

00:59:44.100 --> 00:59:47.000
And we have the incredible
Sarah Simpson on flute.

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:53.430
I think everybody else
is listed in the program.

00:59:53.430 --> 00:59:56.400
But it's fair to point them out.

00:59:56.400 --> 00:59:59.490
This piece will feature
Erik Strand on bass.

00:59:59.490 --> 01:00:01.910
And, Kevin, you're
playing on this as well?

01:00:01.910 --> 01:00:02.410
Yeah.

01:00:02.410 --> 01:00:03.010
You are?

01:00:03.010 --> 01:00:04.210
OK.

01:00:04.210 --> 01:00:05.590
[INAUDIBLE]

01:00:05.590 --> 01:00:06.190
Lovely.

01:00:06.190 --> 01:00:07.330
Here we go.

01:00:07.330 --> 01:00:08.260
"Alexis Dream."

01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:14.695
[MUSIC - "ALEXIS DREAM"]

01:07:57.580 --> 01:08:03.110
Erik Strand, Tony Terrasa,
Kevin Costello's "Alexis Dream."

01:08:09.550 --> 01:08:11.810
I'd like to thank you
all very much for coming.

01:08:11.810 --> 01:08:13.660
It's been such a
pleasure to play for you.

01:08:13.660 --> 01:08:16.720
It's been a very
personal pleasure for me

01:08:16.720 --> 01:08:22.149
to be in this hall again and
to be with these people again.

01:08:22.149 --> 01:08:25.569
They're lovely human
beings and lovely musicians

01:08:25.569 --> 01:08:29.050
and really treasured
every minute with them.

01:08:29.050 --> 01:08:33.100
We are going to close with
yet another original piece,

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:35.560
this time by Brandon Allen.

01:08:35.560 --> 01:08:40.060
It is-- well, it's an original
arrangement of the Bud Powell

01:08:40.060 --> 01:08:42.609
standard "Un Poco Loco."

01:08:42.609 --> 01:08:45.535
And it features Peter
Godart on piano.

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:06.300
Oh, yes.

01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:07.553
I suppose I should.

01:09:07.553 --> 01:09:08.053
OK.

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:16.850
This is a Fred tradition, right?

01:09:16.850 --> 01:09:20.740
OK, that means I have to
remember everybody's name,

01:09:20.740 --> 01:09:21.240
right?

01:09:21.240 --> 01:09:23.279
I'm going to try.

01:09:23.279 --> 01:09:25.620
OK, it's only been
five weeks, or I

01:09:25.620 --> 01:09:29.399
should say seven
rehearsals-- six rehearsals.

01:09:29.399 --> 01:09:29.899
Let's see.

01:09:29.899 --> 01:09:31.191
